  "explanations": [
    {
      "id": "exp-mach-definition",
      "text": "The ratio of flow speed to the speed at which small isentropic disturbances propagate defines the compressibility regime. At low Mach numbers density changes are very small, while near and beyond sonic speed they become comparable to velocity changes.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "sourceId": "nasa-grc-role-of-the-mach-number",
          "quote": "The Mach number is the ratio of the speed of the aircraft, or the speed of the gas, to the speed of sound in the gas."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "exp-sound-speed-equation",
      "text": "For an ideal gas the propagation speed of small disturbances follows from the equation of state and the isentropic relation. The combination shows the sound speed depends on gamma, R, and T.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "sourceId": "nasa-grc-role-of-the-mach-number",
          "quote": "p = rho * R * T"
        },
        {
          "sourceId": "nasa-grc-role-of-the-mach-number",
          "quote": "gamma * R * T = a^2"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "exp-compressibility-shocks",
      "text": "Density variations scale with Mach number squared. Compressibility alters resulting forces because aerodynamic force depends on density, and at higher Mach numbers shock waves can form affecting lift, drag, and downstream conditions.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "sourceId": "nasa-grc-role-of-the-mach-number",
          "quote": "This compressibility effect alters the amount of resulting force on the aircraft since the aerodynamic force depends on the air density."
        },
        {
          "sourceId": "nasa-grc-role-of-the-mach-number",
          "quote": "Sharp disturbances generate shock waves that affect both the lift and drag of the aircraft and the flow conditions downstream of the shock wave."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
